steps.body:
When a user writes in to say their booking failed at about ten past two, you need to find their request among thousands of log lines. A **request id** makes that quick. It's a unique label given to each request, written into every log line the request causes, and sent back to the client in a header. The client can show it on an error screen, and support can search the logs for it. When Roombook calls another service later in the course, it passes the id along, so one id follows the request through every system it touches. That's why it's also called a **correlation id**.

## Read it or make it

The id travels in the `X-Request-Id` header. When a request already has one, from a proxy in front of the API or a client that made its own, Roombook keeps it, so the id matches across systems. Otherwise, it makes a new random one. Either way, the id goes back on the response.

A header is input from outside, so check it before you trust it. Roombook keeps an incoming id only if it's 1 to 64 letters, digits, dots, dashes, and underscores. Anything else is replaced with a fresh id: a header of ten thousand characters, or one with a line break meant to forge an extra line in your logs.

## Request-scoped storage

Code further in needs the id: handlers that log, the error handler, and calls to other services. Passing it as an argument through every function would be clumsy, and a global variable would be wrong, since a server works on many requests at once and a global would mix them up. Every framework has somewhere to keep values for one request only, called **request-scoped storage**.

In Go, it's the request's **context**, a `context.Context` that travels with the request and holds values, deadlines, and a signal for when the client goes away. You don't change a context, you make a new one with a value added, and a new request that carries it. Here is the middleware, in `internal/api/middleware.go`:

```go
type ctxKey int

const requestIDKey ctxKey = 0

var safeID = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,64}$`)

func RequestID(ctx context.Context) string {
	id, _ := ctx.Value(requestIDKey).(string)
	return id
}

func requestID(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		id := r.Header.Get("X-Request-Id")
		if !safeID.MatchString(id) {
			id = rand.Text()
		}
		w.Header().Set("X-Request-Id", id)
		ctx := context.WithValue(r.Context(), requestIDKey, id)
		next.ServeHTTP(w, r.WithContext(ctx))
	})
}
```

The key has its own unexported type, `ctxKey`, so no other package can make a key that collides with it, even by using the same number. `RequestID(ctx)` is how everything else reads the id. `rand.Text()`, from `crypto/rand`, returns 26 random characters, unique enough that two requests will never share one.

## Timing the whole request

A Go middleware can time the request easily, since `next.ServeHTTP` returns when the handler is done. Getting the status is harder: `http.ResponseWriter` has no method that tells you what the handler wrote. So the middleware hands the handler a wrapped writer that notes the status on its way through:

```go
type statusWriter struct {
	http.ResponseWriter
	status int
}

func (w *statusWriter) WriteHeader(status int) {
	if w.status == 0 {
		w.status = status
	}
	w.ResponseWriter.WriteHeader(status)
}

func (w *statusWriter) Write(b []byte) (int, error) {
	if w.status == 0 {
		w.status = http.StatusOK
	}
	return w.ResponseWriter.Write(b)
}

func (w *statusWriter) Unwrap() http.ResponseWriter { return w.ResponseWriter }

func accessLog(log *slog.Logger) func(http.Handler) http.Handler {
	return func(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
		return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
			start := time.Now()
			sw := &statusWriter{ResponseWriter: w}
			next.ServeHTTP(sw, r)
			log.InfoContext(r.Context(), "request",
				"requestId", RequestID(r.Context()),
				"method", r.Method, "path", r.URL.Path,
				"status", sw.status,
				"ms", float64(time.Since(start).Microseconds())/1000)
		})
	}
}
```

Embedding `http.ResponseWriter` in the struct passes every method through, and the two it overrides note the status. A handler that writes a body without calling `WriteHeader` gets 200, so `Write` notes that too. `Unwrap` lets `http.NewResponseController`, which the Redis lesson uses to stream live updates, reach the real writer underneath. The logger is `log/slog` with its JSON handler, so each line is one JSON object a log search tool can filter on. Two requests through the app print:

```text
{"time":"2026-11-21T23:50:55.337366Z","level":"INFO","msg":"request","requestId":"ZA5DVQAVFAXMTCU4OIKFZXMBZ2","method":"GET","path":"/v1/rooms","status":200,"ms":0.006}
{"time":"2026-11-21T23:50:55.345217Z","level":"INFO","msg":"request","requestId":"demo-42","method":"GET","path":"/v1/rooms","status":200,"ms":0.238}
```

## Answering early

A middleware can also decide that a request goes no further. Roombook has a read-only mode for maintenance, like the few minutes while its database moves to a new server. Reads keep working, and anything that would change data gets `503 Service Unavailable`, with a `Retry-After` header that says how many seconds to wait before trying again. Because it's a middleware, it covers every write route at once, including ones added next year.

Here is the read-only switch. It takes the setting and returns the middleware, and for anything but a safe method, it answers and returns without calling `next`:

```go
func readOnly(enabled bool) func(http.Handler) http.Handler {
	return func(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
		return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
			safe := r.Method == http.MethodGet || r.Method == http.MethodHead || r.Method == http.MethodOptions
			if !enabled || safe {
				next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
				return
			}
			w.Header().Set("Retry-After", "600")
			w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
			w.WriteHeader(http.StatusServiceUnavailable)
			w.Write([]byte(`{"status":503,"title":"Roombook is read-only for maintenance"}` + "\n"))
		})
	}
}
```

Headers go on before `WriteHeader`, since the status line and headers leave together once it's called, as the routes lesson showed.

Handlers fail. A database goes away, a bug reads a field of something that isn't there, a library throws an error nobody expected. Without a plan, what the client gets depends on where it failed and on the framework's defaults. It might be an HTML error page from a JSON API, or a **stack trace**, the list of function calls that led to the error, which tells a stranger which libraries and files you use. It might be a connection that simply closes.

Error-handling middleware is the plan. It's one place, at the edge of the pipeline, that every unhandled error reaches, and that turns each one into a response you chose. It has three jobs:

1. **Log the details for yourself**: the error, its stack trace, and the request id, so you can find and fix it.
2. **Answer the client safely**: a 500 with a short, generic message and the request id, and nothing about your code. The client can't fix a bug in the server, and the details would only help someone looking for one.
3. **Answer unknown paths with a 404** in the same JSON shape, instead of the framework's default page.

## Panics, and recovering from them

Go has no exceptions to catch. A Go handler that fails returns or writes its own error, and the next lesson, on validation and errors, gives those errors one central place to go. What Go does have is the **panic**: what happens on a bug like writing to a `nil` map, or reading past the end of a slice. A panic unwinds the goroutine running the handler. The `net/http` server catches it, so one bad request doesn't stop the server, but all it does is log the panic and close the connection, and the client gets no answer at all, as the first example shows.

A recovering middleware turns a panic into a proper 500. It's a method on Roombook's `API` type, so it can use the logger the type holds:

```go
func (a *API) recoverPanics(next http.Handler) http.Handler {
	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		defer func() {
			v := recover()
			if v == nil {
				return
			}
			if v == http.ErrAbortHandler {
				panic(v)
			}
			a.log.ErrorContext(r.Context(), "panic",
				"requestId", RequestID(r.Context()), "err", v)
			w.Header().Set("Content-Type", "application/json")
			w.WriteHeader(http.StatusInternalServerError)
			json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(map[string]any{
				"status": 500, "title": "Internal Server Error",
				"requestId": RequestID(r.Context()),
			})
		}()
		next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
	})
}
```

`recover()` only works inside a deferred function, so the check goes in a `defer` that runs as the handler unwinds. `http.ErrAbortHandler` is the one panic to leave alone: a handler panics with it on purpose to drop a connection, and the server expects to see it. The middleware sits inside `accessLog`, so the 500 still gets its log line with the status.

## Unknown paths

The mux already answers unknown paths with 404, and known paths with the wrong method with 405, but as plain text: `404 page not found`, as the second example shows. The next lesson, on validation and errors, adds one more small middleware that turns those into JSON, using the same wrapped-writer trick as `statusWriter`.

That lesson also turns every error into the standard format Roombook uses, and teaches one central handler to answer 400, 409, and 422 when a request breaks the rules. For now, `recoverPanics` covers the case every API has from its first day: a bug.
